  "output": "I now have a complete picture. Two important framing notes before the findings: the **engine layer (`engine/`) is entirely unimplemented** — every pipeline function (`normalize`, `block`, `match`, `detect`, `resolve`, all of `db.py`) is a `raise NotImplementedError` stub per the Phase 1/2/3 build order in `AGENTS.md`. There's no executable logic there yet, so N+1/leak/redundant-computation analysis doesn't apply to it — flagging that now saves rediscovering it later. Everything below is real, verified against the code, and scoped to `web/`, which is fully built out.\n\n## 1. N+1 query patterns\n\n**None found — this is already handled well.** `web/lib/server/conflicts.ts` and `export.ts` explicitly batch: one 5-table join for the queue page, one grouped `countChildrenByParent` query for the whole page instead of per-row (`conflicts.ts:186-190`), and counts run via `Promise.all`. The code even has inline comments justifying this (\"Three round trips, not three hundred\").\n\n**But: missing indexes will turn these good queries into slow ones at scale.** `web/lib/db/schema.ts` gives `entities` a composite index, but nothing else has one:\n\n```ts\n// conflicts, matches, resolutions, audit_log — no index() at all\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\"conflicts\", { runId: uuid(...).references(...), ... });\nexport const matches = pgTable(\"matches\", { runId: uuid(...).references(...), ... });\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\"resolutions\", { conflictId: uuid(...).references(...), ... });\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\"audit_log\", { runId: uuid(...).references(...), ... });\n```\n\nEvery queue/audit/export query filters or joins on exactly these columns (`conflicts.runId`, `matches.entityAId/entityBId`, `resolutions.conflictId`, `auditLog.runId`). Postgres does **not** auto-index foreign keys. At a few hundred conflicts this is invisible; at the volumes `AGENTS.md` describes (\"thousands of rows across entities, matches, conflicts and resolutions\") every queue page load, audit page load, and export becomes a sequential scan.\n\n```ts\n// web/lib/db/schema.ts — add to each table definition\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\"conflicts\", {...}, (table) => [\n  index(\"conflicts_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n]);\nexport const matches = pgTable(\"matches\", {...}, (table) => [\n  index(\"matches_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n  index(\"matches_entity_a_idx\").on(table.entityAId),\n  index(\"matches_entity_b_idx\").on(table.entityBId),\n]);\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\"resolutions\", {...}, (table) => [\n  uniqueIndex(\"resolutions_conflict_id_idx\").on(table.conflictId), // also closes the double-join gap conflicts.ts:63 already flags\n]);\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\"audit_log\", {...}, (table) => [\n  index(\"audit_log_run_idx\").on(table.runId),\n]);\n```\n\nThe `resolutions.conflictId` one should be a **unique** index, not just a regular one — `conflicts.ts:62-66` already documents that a second resolution row per conflict would silently double every count and duplicate queue rows. Worth doing in the same migration.\n\n## 2. Unnecessary re-renders (React)\n\n**Ledger view re-renders the whole page on selection move.** In `conflict-queue.tsx`, `j`/`k` navigation changes `selectedIndex`, which is passed as a new `selected` boolean + new `onSelect`/`submit` closures to *every* `ConflictDiffRow` in the list (up to `QUEUE_PAGE_SIZE = 100` rows), and none of them are memoized:\n\n```tsx\n{rows.map((row, index) => (\n  <ConflictDiffRow\n    key={row.conflictId}\n    row={row}\n    selected={index === clamped}          // changes for 2 rows, re-renders all 100\n    onSelect={() => setSelectedIndex(index)}\n    submit={submitFor(row.conflictId)}     // new closure every render\n    ...\n```\n\nOnly two rows' visual state actually changes per keystroke (the old and new selection). Fix:\n\n```tsx\nexport const ConflictDiffRow = React.memo(function ConflictDiffRow({ ... }) { ... });\n```\n```tsx\nconst submitFor = useCallback(\n  (conflictId: string): ReviewSubmit => (action) => submit(conflictId, action),\n  [submit],\n);\n```\n`submitFor` is already stable via `useCallback`, but it returns a *new* closure on every call — so even with `memo`, every row's `submit` prop looks new every render. Cache per-row functions with a `Map` keyed by `conflictId`, or pass `conflictId` down and let each row call a stable `submit(conflictId, action)` directly instead of a per-row curried function.\n\n**The keydown listener resubscribes on every selection change.** `useEffect` in `conflict-queue.tsx:79-132` depends on `[move, selected, submit]`, and `selected` is a new object every render (`rows[clamped]`), so `window.addEventListener`/`removeEventListener` fires on every j/k press:\n\n```tsx\n// current — re-subscribes the global listener every navigation\nuseEffect(() => { ... }, [move, selected, submit]);\n```\n```tsx\n// fix — read current selection from a ref, subscribe once\nconst selectedRef = useRef(selected);\nselectedRef.current = selected;\nuseEffect(() => {\n  function onKeyDown(event: KeyboardEvent) {\n    const selected = selectedRef.current;\n    // ...\n  }\n  window.addEventListener(\"keydown\", onKeyDown);\n  return () => window.removeEventListener(\"keydown\", onKeyDown);\n}, [move, submit]); // stable deps\n```\nNot a leak (cleanup is correct) — just unnecessary DOM listener churn on every keypress.\n\n**`RuleRow` does O(n) lookups per row → O(n²) per render.** In `ruleset-editor.tsx:166-179`:\n\n```tsx\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);            // O(n)\n  return (\n    <RuleRow position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1} ... />  // O(n) again\n```\n\nRulesets are small (tens of rows) so this is not a real bottleneck today, but it's redundant computation that's trivial to remove — build an index map once:\n\n```tsx\nconst indexById = new Map(rules.map((r, i) => [r.id, i]));\nconst positionById = new Map(ordered.map((r, i) => [r.id, i + 1]));\n{ordered.map((rule) => (\n  <RuleRow position={positionById.get(rule.id)!} onChange={(patch) => update(indexById.get(rule.id)!, patch)} ... />\n))}\n```\n\n## 3. Caching opportunities\n\n**`getRuleset(runId's rulesetId)` is fetched fresh on every queue page load despite being immutable.** `rulesets.ts` documents this itself: \"Rulesets are VERSIONED, NEVER EDITED IN PLACE\" — once written, a `(id)` row never changes. `loadQueue` (`conflicts.ts:226`) calls `getRuleset(run.rulesetId)` on every request. This is a textbook case for React's `cache()` (per-request dedup) plus a longer-lived cache since the data is provably immutable:\n\n```ts\n// web/lib/server/rulesets.ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\n\nexport const getRuleset = cache(async (id: string): Promise<Ruleset | null> => {\n  await connection();\n  const [row] = await getDb().select().from(rulesets).where(eq(rulesets.id, id)).limit(1);\n  return row ? rulesetSchema.parse(row) : null;\n});\n```\n`cache()` at minimum dedupes it within a single request tree (useful once this is read from more than one place per render, e.g. queue + export on the same navigation); since the underlying row is immutable by construction, this is also a safe candidate for `unstable_cache` with no revalidation trigger needed beyond a new version's own uuid.\n\n**`getRunDetail` / a run's static fields (`sourceAId`, `rulesetId`, `startedAt`) never change after `finishedAt` is set**, but `getQueuePage`, `getReviewTarget`, and audit pages all re-fetch the full joined run row per request. Once `run.status` is terminal, wrapping the joined lookup in `unstable_cache` keyed by `runId` (with no revalidation, since a finished run's row is append-only from there) would remove a join from every subsequent queue/audit/export request for that run.\n\n**`countSourcesBySide()` re-lists and re-filters all sources on every call** (`sources.ts:24-31`) rather than a single grouped `count() ... group by kind` query — small today, but redundant work worth folding into one query if sources ever get numerous:\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const rows = await getDb()\n    .select({ kind: sources.kind, total: count() })\n    .from(sources)\n    .groupBy(sources.kind);\n  // reduce rows into { crm, warehouse, synthetic }\n}\n```\n\n## 4. Memory leaks\n\n**None found.** Every subscription in the client components is cleaned up correctly:\n- `RunProgress`'s `useQuery` stops polling once `isTerminalRunStatus` is true (`run-live.tsx:56-59`) and TanStack Query unmounts its own interval.\n- `Elapsed`'s `setInterval` is cleared in the effect's return (`run-live.tsx:106-107`).\n- `ConflictQueue`'s `keydown` listener is removed on unmount/dep-change (`conflict-queue.tsx:131`) — see the re-render note above; it's inefficient, not leaking.\n- `getDb()` caches the Postgres client on `globalThis` specifically to avoid opening a new pooled connection on every HMR reload (`db/index.ts:19-24`) — correct pattern, not a leak.\n\n## 5. Redundant computations\n\n- `RuleRow`'s double `indexOf` (above) — real but low-impact given ruleset size.\n- `countSourcesBySide()` re-filtering the full source list three times in memory (above) — real but low-impact given source count.\n- `diffSegments` in `diff-chars.ts` is an intentional O(n) prefix/suffix scan, computed once per `DiffValue` render with no memoization — for row values in the tens-of-characters range this is fine; if `value`/`other` payloads ever grow much larger, wrap in `useMemo` keyed on `[value, other]`.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nThe web layer is already written with N+1 avoidance and query batching as explicit design goals (the code comments make this legible), so there's no low-hanging fruit there. The highest-value, lowest-risk fix is **adding indexes on `conflicts.runId`, `matches.runId/entityAId/entityBId`, `resolutions.conflictId` (unique), and `audit_log.runId`** — that's a one-file Drizzle migration and directly protects the queries that already exist. The React findings (row memoization, stable per-row callbacks, the keydown listener) matter once queues regularly hit the 100-row page size with frequent j/k navigation; today they're inefficiencies, not visible bugs. The engine layer has no code yet to optimize — that analysis becomes relevant once Phase 2/3 (`normalize`, `blocking`, `detect`, `agent/graph.py`) are implemented.\n",
